265
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:54,790
I wish to report to
the intergalactic council

266
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:58,280
that my carrier has arrived
on station as programmed.

267
00:10:58,290 --> 00:10:59,790
I await further orders.

268
00:10:59,790 --> 00:11:00,290
Clear your throat.

269
00:11:00,790 --> 00:11:03,790
You may now be advised
of your assignment.

270
00:11:03,790 --> 00:11:07,800
The primary objective of
your mission to planet Earth

271
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:10,300
is to establish a colony within

272
00:11:10,300 --> 00:11:12,800
the advanced society
of the planet.

273
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:13,800
Okay.

274
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:18,310
This is necessary in
order that we may expand

275
00:11:18,310 --> 00:11:22,810
our galaxy domination
program in the near future.

276
00:11:22,810 --> 00:11:24,820
I understand the
importance of the mission.

277
00:11:24,810 --> 00:11:25,810
Yeah right.

278
00:11:25,810 --> 00:11:26,810
You will accomplish

279
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:29,320
this infiltration
of the Earth society

280
00:11:29,320 --> 00:11:31,820
with the aid of Professor
Vaughn Dornheimer,

281
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:32,820
(audience laughing)

282
00:11:32,820 --> 00:11:35,330
a renowned scientist
of that society.

283
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:35,820
And a good friend.

284
00:11:35,820 --> 00:11:37,820
I understand, master.

285
00:11:37,830 --> 00:11:41,830
If you are detected,
if you fail in any way

286
00:11:41,830 --> 00:11:43,330
It will go on your
permanent record.

287
00:11:43,330 --> 00:11:45,340
You will be destroyed.

288
00:11:45,330 --> 00:11:46,830
I will not fail.

289
00:11:46,840 --> 00:11:48,340
Much.

290
00:11:48,340 --> 00:11:51,340
Agent Kolos, you may
now take your place

291
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:54,350
before the teletransporter.

292
00:11:54,340 --> 00:11:56,840
We will contact you
after you've had

293
00:11:56,850 --> 00:11:59,850
sufficient time to
accomplish your mission.

294
00:11:59,850 --> 00:12:00,850
Okay? Buh-bye now.

295
00:12:00,850 --> 00:12:03,350
Buh-bye.

296
00:12:03,350 --> 00:12:06,360
Your celebrity partner
will be Nipsey Russel!

297
00:12:06,350 --> 00:12:07,350
(cheering)

298
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:11,360
Sprinkle a day makes
Richard Kiel go away.

299
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:15,370
Did I leave the iron on?

300
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:19,860
Oh no, he got beamed into
the "Song of the South".

301
00:12:19,870 --> 00:12:21,870
♪ Zip a dee do dah

302
00:12:21,870 --> 00:12:23,870
I'm huge.

303
00:12:23,870 --> 00:12:25,880
We visited Anthony Robbins

304
00:12:25,870 --> 00:12:33,870
in his palatial home in Del Mar.

305
00:12:33,880 --> 00:12:36,390
I begin my tour of
Southern California

306
00:12:36,380 --> 00:12:38,880
with a quick trip to Greystone.

307
00:12:38,890 --> 00:12:40,390
This is "Beverly Hills Cop",

308
00:12:40,390 --> 00:12:43,890
only the slow, white version.

309
00:12:43,890 --> 00:12:44,900
So, uh, what is his mission?

310
00:12:44,890 --> 00:12:46,390
I dunno.

311
00:12:46,390 --> 00:12:48,890
Selling band candy or something.

312
00:12:48,900 --> 00:12:49,900
You rang?

313
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:53,900
Oh wait, I'm not
Ted Cassidy, sorry.

314
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:54,910
(audience yelling)

315
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:56,900
Can I help you, sir?

316
00:12:56,910 --> 00:12:58,410
I wish to see
Professor Dornheimer.

317
00:12:58,410 --> 00:13:00,910
I'm very sorry sir, the
professor's very busy.

318
00:13:00,910 --> 00:13:02,910
You'll have to make
an appointment.

319
00:13:02,910 --> 00:13:05,420
I wish to see the
professor at once.

320
00:13:05,410 --> 00:13:06,410
(dramatic music)

321
00:13:06,410 --> 00:13:09,910
(audience mimicking choking)

322
00:13:09,920 --> 00:13:14,430
He must have read
"Getting to Yes."

323
00:13:14,420 --> 00:13:19,920
Looks like the Ponderosa.

324
00:13:19,930 --> 00:13:24,440
Excuse me, where's
the little giants' room?

325
00:13:24,430 --> 00:13:29,430
(Crow singing
"Bonanza" theme song)

326
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:30,940
Oh? Hmm.

327
00:13:30,940 --> 00:13:34,950
(gentle piano music)

328
00:13:35,440 --> 00:13:38,440
Kate Mulgrew is Mrs. Chopin.

329
00:13:38,450 --> 00:13:39,950
Uh, dear Penthouse,

330
00:13:39,950 --> 00:13:45,460
I am a child from a small
Midwestern satellite, and, uh...

331
00:13:45,450 --> 00:13:49,450
I'm here for my piano
lesson, Madame Sousatzka.

332
00:13:49,460 --> 00:13:51,460
Oh Silky, have you
been rolling in the carp?

333
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:52,960
Thor?

334
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:54,970
Is that you?

335
00:13:54,960 --> 00:13:56,460
Oh, she's blind.

336
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:57,460
Well that explains
the decorating.

337
00:13:57,470 --> 00:13:58,470
(Tom laughing nervously)

338
00:13:58,470 --> 00:13:59,970
Is that you professor?

339
00:13:59,970 --> 00:14:02,470
Uh no, it's me, Gilligan.

340
00:14:02,470 --> 00:14:06,480
Hey, who knows where
that hand has been!

341
00:14:06,470 --> 00:14:07,470
Sleep!

342
00:14:07,480 --> 00:14:08,480
Oh well.

343
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:09,480
Who is it?

344
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:10,980
Who's there?

345
00:14:10,980 --> 00:14:12,480
I'm Dr. Kolos.

346
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:14,990
A colleague of
Professor Dornheimer.

347
00:14:14,980 --> 00:14:16,980
I have traveled a great
distance to see him.

348
00:14:16,990 --> 00:14:18,490
Geez, somebody
get her a sandwich.

349
00:14:18,490 --> 00:14:20,490
The professor is
in his laboratory.

350
00:14:20,490 --> 00:14:21,990
Was he expecting you, Doctor?

351
00:14:22,490 --> 00:14:24,500
No, he was not
expecting my visit.

352
00:14:24,490 --> 00:14:25,990
Oh, but he'll be
pleasantly surprised.

353
00:14:25,990 --> 00:14:28,990
Well, he doesn't like to be
disturbed while he's working.

354
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
Perhaps you could phone in the
morning for an appointment?

355
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:32,500
Or if you care to wait--

356
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:34,510
It is imperative
that I see him at once.

357
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:36,000
It's a stripogram.

358
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,000
I assure you, it
will be alright.

359
00:14:39,010 --> 00:14:42,010
My business with your uncle
is of extreme importance.

360
00:14:42,010 --> 00:14:43,510
Come on, I'm huge, humor me.

361
00:14:43,510 --> 00:14:45,520
You may trust in me, Lisa.

362
00:14:45,510 --> 00:14:47,010
How did you know my name?

363
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:49,520
I have been informed
of your presence here.

364
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:52,520
But I had not expected
you to be so beautiful.

365
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:55,030
Look, I'm from another
planet, the meter's running.

366
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:58,020
I will show you to the
laboratory, Dr. Kolos.

367
00:14:58,030 --> 00:15:08,740
(audience hooting
and catcalling)

368
00:15:08,740 --> 00:15:12,240
It's quite alright,
Doctor, I can find my way.

369
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:14,240
You see, I grew up here.

370
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:19,750
I know every inch of
this house by heart.

371
00:15:19,750 --> 00:15:21,750
Ooh, new inch.

372
00:15:22,250 --> 00:15:24,250
Uh, I guess you know
that you're leading me

373
00:15:24,250 --> 00:15:25,760
into the bathroom, ha ha.

374
00:15:25,750 --> 00:15:27,750
It's the blind
leading the bland.

375
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:29,260
And this is the kitchen,

376
00:15:29,260 --> 00:15:31,760
here's the new stove we
just ordered from Sears.

377
00:15:31,760 --> 00:15:35,270
(dramatic music)

378
00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:39,760
And, uh, this is my room,
it's done in pinks.

379
00:15:39,770 --> 00:15:44,310
Marilyn's date is
here and he's ugly!

380
00:15:44,810 --> 00:15:49,810
This is my velvet
underground, like it?

381
00:15:49,810 --> 00:15:54,320
Looks like they forgot
to strike the time tunnel set.

382
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:55,820
Oh great, look who came in,

383
00:15:55,820 --> 00:15:58,320
and without an
appointment, I might add.

384
00:15:58,320 --> 00:15:59,820
It's Grant Tinker!

385
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:04,330
(dramatic music)

386
00:16:04,330 --> 00:16:06,830
Professor Dornheimer.

387
00:16:06,830 --> 00:16:08,330
I am Dr. Kolos.

388
00:16:08,330 --> 00:16:12,340
The God of Hellfire
and I bring you...

389
00:16:12,330 --> 00:16:14,330
(audience yelling)

390
00:16:14,340 --> 00:16:15,840
Oh my God, you're huge.

391
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:17,840
I'm sure I do
not know you, sir.

392
00:16:17,840 --> 00:16:19,340
But I think I could like you.

393
00:16:19,340 --> 00:16:20,840
Perhaps you made a mistake.

394
00:16:20,840 --> 00:16:23,350
There is no mistake,
Professor Dornheimer.

395
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:26,340
I have come to assist
you in your experiment.

396
00:16:26,350 --> 00:16:27,350
Uh, but we're not hiring.

397
00:16:27,350 --> 00:16:31,360
I don't need your assistance.

398
00:16:31,350 --> 00:16:32,850
(laughing) But then again,

399
00:16:32,850 --> 00:16:36,850
I could use a huge
man and a, oh.

400
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:38,860
But you do, sir.

401
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:41,370
Your theories are correct.

402
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:43,360
But your technique is wrong.

403
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:44,360
Everything you know is wrong!

404
00:16:44,370 --> 00:16:46,370
In this manner,

405
00:16:46,370 --> 00:16:49,870
you will not reach your
goal for many years.

406
00:16:49,870 --> 00:16:51,880
Together, professor,

407
00:16:51,870 --> 00:16:56,500
we shall create an
android in a few days.

408
00:16:56,510 --> 00:16:58,520
Besides, I brought
my own lab coat.

409
00:16:58,510 --> 00:17:00,010
I have important work to do.

410
00:17:00,010 --> 00:17:02,510
I will not be distracted
by a total stranger

411
00:17:02,520 --> 00:17:04,020
with fantastic ideas.

412
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:05,020
Thanks, I think.

413
00:17:05,020 --> 00:17:06,520
But you will, sir.

414
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:07,520
(dramatic music)

415
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:10,530
Pons and Fleischmann,
the early years.

416
00:17:10,530 --> 00:17:12,030
I command it.

417
00:17:12,030 --> 00:17:13,530
Oh, well in that case.

418
00:17:13,530 --> 00:17:15,530
Who are you?

419
00:17:15,530 --> 00:17:16,530
I am Kolos.

420
00:17:16,530 --> 00:17:18,040
And Diet Kolos.

421
00:17:18,030 --> 00:17:20,030
(dramatic music)

422
00:17:20,030 --> 00:17:22,030
Kevin McHale, no!

423
00:17:22,040 --> 00:17:23,540
I am your master.

424
00:17:23,540 --> 00:17:24,540
(sudden trumpeting)

425
00:17:24,540 --> 00:17:26,540
Oh, brother's got
his own horn section.

426
00:17:26,540 --> 00:17:28,050
Cool.

427
00:17:28,040 --> 00:17:34,480
("Love Theme from MST3K")

428
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:40,480
Next week on
"Human Duplicators".

429
00:17:40,490 --> 00:17:42,490
Good morning sir.

430
00:17:42,490 --> 00:17:45,500
Ah, sit and spin.

431
00:17:45,490 --> 00:17:50,990
Kind of a high-security
Budgetel here, huh?

432
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:55,010
Oh he's lucky to get
a parking spot so close.

433
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:56,500
He's got special clearance

434
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:58,500
for the hospitality suite.

435
00:17:58,510 --> 00:17:59,510
Well, you know, he
could have walked down

436
00:17:59,510 --> 00:18:02,010
those high security
steps over there.

437
00:18:02,010 --> 00:18:04,010
Yes, well, I worked at NASA

438
00:18:04,010 --> 00:18:11,020
back when we were
next to Cost Cutters!

439
00:18:11,020 --> 00:18:12,520
Dr. Munson, good morning, sir.

440
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:14,530
Hey, it's the
Maytag repairman.

441
00:18:14,520 --> 00:18:17,020
Came in a little
early, couldn't sleep?

442
00:18:17,030 --> 00:18:20,030
I'm here for the Mr.
Drysdale audition. (laughing)

443
00:18:20,030 --> 00:18:22,030
That's right, couldn't sleep.

444
00:18:22,030 --> 00:18:23,030
Perchance to dream.

445
00:18:23,030 --> 00:18:24,030
Oh, wait, sir,

446
00:18:24,530 --> 00:18:27,530
I'm sorry, Doctor, but you
know our security rules.

447
00:18:27,540 --> 00:18:29,040
Yes, of course.

448
00:18:29,040 --> 00:18:32,040
Hop like a bunny.

449
00:18:32,040 --> 00:18:33,040
Okay, let's see.

450
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:38,050
Woozle, whose name is
Peanut, there you go.

451
00:18:38,050 --> 00:18:39,550
There, is that better?

452
00:18:39,550 --> 00:18:41,050
Yes sir, thank you.

453
00:18:41,050 --> 00:18:42,550
Wait, sit and spin,
hey wait a minute!

454
00:18:43,050 --> 00:18:44,050
Computer room, eh?

455
00:18:44,050 --> 00:18:48,060
Boing.

456
00:18:48,060 --> 00:18:50,060
There better be a
continental breakfast,

457
00:18:50,060 --> 00:18:51,560
that's all I gotta say.

458
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:53,560
Why did he have to
sign in to go outside?

459
00:18:53,560 --> 00:18:55,070
It's California.

460
00:18:55,060 --> 00:18:58,060
(dramatic music)

461
00:18:58,070 --> 00:19:00,570
Oh, boy. (laughing)
That makes it good.

462
00:19:00,570 --> 00:19:02,570
It's "The Pawnbroker" meets

463
00:19:02,570 --> 00:19:07,580
"It's a Mad, Mad, Mad, Mad
World," the restored version.

464
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:09,580
Hmm.

465
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:12,080
All I have to do is... hey.

466
00:19:12,080 --> 00:19:13,080
Wait a minute.

467
00:19:13,080 --> 00:19:15,090
I only have so many years left,

468
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:16,580
why am I wasting
my time in this film?

469
00:19:16,580 --> 00:19:17,080
Agh!

470
00:19:17,090 --> 00:19:18,090
(alarm sounding)

471
00:19:18,090 --> 00:19:20,090
Hey, Fred Flinstone's
running somewhere.

472
00:19:20,090 --> 00:19:21,090
(Tom impersonating
Flinstones' car motor)

473
00:19:21,090 --> 00:19:23,590
I wet 'em!

474
00:19:23,590 --> 00:19:27,600
Oh, got to get to bio!

475
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:29,100
This is what they
get for leaving

476
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:32,600
national security secrets in
a high school in Pasadena.

477
00:19:32,600 --> 00:19:36,610
It's the office of Daniel
Ellsberg's electrician.

478
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:38,600
I'll stand still,
he won't see me.

479
00:19:38,610 --> 00:19:39,610
Oh, Dr. Munson!

480
00:19:39,610 --> 00:19:40,610
Where's your AV card?

481
00:19:40,610 --> 00:19:43,610
I thought you were
in the computer room.

482
00:19:43,610 --> 00:19:44,620
What happened here?

483
00:19:45,110 --> 00:19:47,610
Nothing to be
concerned about, Johnson.

484
00:19:47,620 --> 00:19:49,620
- It's Olson.
- Nothing whatever.

485
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:51,620
Glad everything's alright!

486
00:19:51,620 --> 00:19:54,630
Sounds like a promotion.

487
00:19:54,620 --> 00:19:58,620
Dr. Heimlich, the early years.

488
00:19:58,630 --> 00:20:01,630
Now I can take all
the penny nails I want.

489
00:20:01,630 --> 00:20:03,130
(evil laughing)

490
00:20:03,130 --> 00:20:05,640
(dramatic music)

491
00:20:05,630 --> 00:20:11,630
All classes dismissed,
school's out forever!

492
00:20:11,640 --> 00:20:14,650
Hey, look, there's
a doorknob, buddy!

493
00:20:14,640 --> 00:20:16,640
Jerk.

494
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:17,640
Dr. Munson!

495
00:20:17,650 --> 00:20:19,150
I think I love you!

496
00:20:19,150 --> 00:20:21,650
(gunfire)

497
00:20:21,650 --> 00:20:22,650
(gunfire)

498
00:20:22,650 --> 00:20:26,660
Greenie's Stick-M-Caps
won't stop him!

499
00:20:26,650 --> 00:20:27,650
Alright, okay, forget it,

500
00:20:27,660 --> 00:20:30,160
I'll sign you out then,
okay, bye.

501
00:20:30,160 --> 00:20:34,670
Stop in the name of
Tom Bodett!

502
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:36,160
(mimicking police radio)
1-Adam-12, 1-Adam-12...

503
00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:43,160
See the man, government secrets
stolen from the Motel 6.

504
00:20:43,170 --> 00:20:45,180
You know, there's one thing
I can't stand, Steve.

505
00:20:45,170 --> 00:20:49,170
- What's that, Wayne?
- Towel thieves.

506
00:20:49,180 --> 00:20:55,190
Need a room, please.

507
00:20:55,180 --> 00:20:56,680
Tenspeed and Brown Shoe!

508
00:20:56,680 --> 00:20:58,180
National Intelligence Agency.

509
00:20:58,190 --> 00:21:01,690
- NIA, this might be a story!
- Wish we were reporters.

510
00:21:01,690 --> 00:21:04,190
You tell me. I only know
what I read in your papers.

511
00:21:04,190 --> 00:21:05,200
Well, what does he mean
by that?

512
00:21:05,190 --> 00:21:08,260
It's simple. Something's up,
so he doesn't wanna talk.

513
00:21:08,260 --> 00:21:10,260
They never do,
those guys from NIA

514
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:11,760
Might as well forget about it.

515
00:21:11,770 --> 00:21:14,270
Oh, not me! If there's a
story here, I'm gonna get it!

516
00:21:14,270 --> 00:21:15,270
With my accordion!

517
00:21:15,270 --> 00:21:16,770
Sure.

518
00:21:16,770 --> 00:21:19,280
Officer Krupke,
I'm down on my knees...

519
00:21:19,270 --> 00:21:21,270
Thank you, constable.

520
00:21:21,280 --> 00:21:22,780
Oh, he's a quart low.

521
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:24,280
Why should I hurry?

522
00:21:24,280 --> 00:21:26,280
You're doing my work for me.

523
00:21:26,280 --> 00:21:27,780
Mickey Spillane?

524
00:21:27,780 --> 00:21:29,290
I can't have a
quiet homicide anymore

525
00:21:29,280 --> 00:21:31,280
without you NIA
boys being involved.

526
00:21:31,290 --> 00:21:33,290
You pull that suit
off the Riddler?

527
00:21:33,290 --> 00:21:35,790
We're dealing with
an espionage ring.

528
00:21:35,790 --> 00:21:37,290
It might be just that, Shaw.

529
00:21:37,290 --> 00:21:38,300
Dick Clark!

530
00:21:38,290 --> 00:21:39,790
What'd they take this time?

531
00:21:39,790 --> 00:21:42,290
Same as before,
transistors, dials,

532
00:21:42,300 --> 00:21:44,800
laser crystals,
things like that.

533
00:21:44,800 --> 00:21:47,800
I can't believe I loved him.

534
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:49,310
And the suspect?

535
00:21:49,300 --> 00:21:50,800
Eh, seemed like a nice guy.

536
00:21:50,810 --> 00:21:52,310
Trusted member of the staff,

537
00:21:52,310 --> 00:21:54,310
character above
suspicion as usual?

538
00:21:54,310 --> 00:21:57,310
Same, Dr. Munson,
head physicist.

539
00:21:57,310 --> 00:21:59,820
Three times in the
past three months.

540
00:21:59,810 --> 00:22:01,810
- That adds up to something...
- Well here he is!

541
00:22:01,820 --> 00:22:03,820
...like espionage in our books.

542
00:22:03,820 --> 00:22:05,820
Uh, you're
kneeling on his head.

543
00:22:05,820 --> 00:22:08,330
Looks like this Dr. Munson
was the strong man type.

544
00:22:08,320 --> 00:22:09,320
Dr. Munson Honeydew.

545
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:09,820
You're wrong.

546
00:22:09,820 --> 00:22:11,320
I've got news for you,
Glenn boy,

547
00:22:11,330 --> 00:22:13,830
that man's some
kind of a superman.

548
00:22:13,830 --> 00:22:17,330
The guard outside fired four
shots at him, point blank,

549
00:22:17,330 --> 00:22:18,340
never even fazed him.

550
00:22:18,330 --> 00:22:20,160
And to top that,
he busted through

551
00:22:20,670 --> 00:22:22,170
a two inch door out there.

552
00:22:22,170 --> 00:22:23,170
Drinkin' before noon again?

553
00:22:23,170 --> 00:22:25,680
Hate to be the man that
tries to bring him in.

554
00:22:25,670 --> 00:22:27,670
I want to take a
look at his files.

555
00:22:27,680 --> 00:22:29,180
Where's his office?

556
00:22:29,180 --> 00:22:30,680
Across the hall,
I'll show you.

557
00:22:30,680 --> 00:22:33,180
Next to the ice machine.

558
00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:34,180
Lieutenant Shaw?

559
00:22:34,680 --> 00:22:37,190
The highway patrol is
calling in from Stone Canyon.

560
00:22:37,180 --> 00:22:39,180
Eric Estrada is on his way.

561
00:22:39,190 --> 00:22:40,690
(dramatic music)

562
00:22:40,690 --> 00:22:46,700
Hmm. This wheat looks
just about ready to harvest.

563
00:22:46,690 --> 00:22:49,190
Who you gonna call?
(laughs)

564
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:56,710
Space Research Corporation
and Motor Lodge.

565
00:22:57,200 --> 00:22:58,200
Get me Broderick Crawford.

566
00:22:58,210 --> 00:22:59,710
This is Shaw,
let's have it.

567
00:22:59,710 --> 00:23:02,210
Lieutenant, we found the car,
but your man's vanished.

568
00:23:02,210 --> 00:23:03,710
For the moment, anyway.

569
00:23:03,710 --> 00:23:06,220
We've checked and double-checked
the canyon road, not a trace.

570
00:23:06,210 --> 00:23:09,680
Well, keep checking.
Can't get far, he's wounded.

571
00:23:09,680 --> 00:23:11,180
We won't be able
to search too well

572
00:23:11,190 --> 00:23:13,190
until the morning
fog lifts, sir.

573
00:23:13,190 --> 00:23:14,690
Well, do the best you can.

574
00:23:14,690 --> 00:23:16,190
My wife.

575
00:23:16,190 --> 00:23:17,690
He doesn't have
much of a chance.

576
00:23:17,690 --> 00:23:19,200
Don't take bets on it.

577
00:23:19,190 --> 00:23:20,690
Come on, let's take a look
at those files.

578
00:23:20,700 --> 00:23:22,700
Hey, it's Foreman and Clark!

579
00:23:22,700 --> 00:23:24,700
Who did what to whom,
and why?

580
00:23:24,700 --> 00:23:26,700
You'll have to ask him,
it's his case.

581
00:23:26,700 --> 00:23:28,210
I'm just here to assist.

582
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:30,200
Here we go again, kid.
The old ball game.

583
00:23:30,200 --> 00:23:31,700
Alright, Mr. Martin,
what's the score?

584
00:23:31,710 --> 00:23:34,210
You want all the facts and
nothing but the facts, right?

585
00:23:34,210 --> 00:23:35,210
(all)
So help us God.

586
00:23:35,710 --> 00:23:36,710
My office will issue a
statement for the press

587
00:23:36,710 --> 00:23:38,220
as soon as we have those facts.

588
00:23:38,210 --> 00:23:39,210
Come on,
give us a break, will ya?

589
00:23:39,210 --> 00:23:42,710
Sorry, that's the way it is.

590
00:23:42,720 --> 00:23:45,720
Say, are those
Bugle Boy jeans?

591
00:23:46,220 --> 00:23:50,160
And so, Hercules entered
the realm of the Minotaur.

592
00:23:50,160 --> 00:23:53,130
I think it's my
battery again.

593
00:23:53,130 --> 00:23:57,130
Uh, hope you brought the buns.

594
00:23:57,130 --> 00:24:00,640
- Any way to get down there?
- Uh, donkeys?

595
00:24:00,630 --> 00:24:02,630
It's quite a hike, though.

596
00:24:02,640 --> 00:24:03,640
Whose place is that?

597
00:24:04,140 --> 00:24:06,640
I think I know, his house
is in the village, though.

598
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:09,650
There's a funny old guy,
wants complete privacy.

599
00:24:09,640 --> 00:24:11,140
Supposed to be a famous
doctor or something.

600
00:24:11,150 --> 00:24:12,650
Well, takes all kinds.
(laughs)

601
00:24:12,650 --> 00:24:15,650
Let's go.

602
00:24:15,650 --> 00:24:18,660
Can I ride your back?
I wanna ride on your back.

603
00:24:18,650 --> 00:24:25,150
C'mon.

604
00:24:25,160 --> 00:24:27,160
Bobby? Cindy?

605
00:24:27,160 --> 00:24:30,170
To be continued.

606
00:24:30,160 --> 00:24:33,160
Hey, let's ask this
old guy taking a nap.

607
00:24:33,170 --> 00:24:37,170
Ooh, kids come down
here and drink lime vodka.

608
00:24:37,170 --> 00:24:38,680
I guess he sort of lost his way.

609
00:24:38,670 --> 00:24:39,670
In all that fog this morning,

610
00:24:39,670 --> 00:24:41,170
he couldn't see
the edge of the cliff.

611
00:24:41,180 --> 00:24:44,180
We gotta go.

612
00:24:44,680 --> 00:24:47,180
Come on mister,
you can't sleep here.

613
00:24:47,180 --> 00:24:51,320
(dramatic music)

614
00:24:51,320 --> 00:24:53,320
No Munson.

615
00:24:53,320 --> 00:25:00,800
(doors creak and whir)

616
00:25:00,800 --> 00:25:03,800
Okay, everybody ready
with the big craft project?

617
00:25:03,800 --> 00:25:04,300
- Yeah.
- Uh huh.

618
00:25:04,300 --> 00:25:06,800
Oh yes, yes, yes, yes, oh.

619
00:25:06,800 --> 00:25:07,800
- Alright.
- Oh yes, yes.

620
00:25:07,800 --> 00:25:09,810
Okay and that
craft project was?

621
00:25:09,800 --> 00:25:12,800
To make our very own spaceship

622
00:25:12,810 --> 00:25:17,310
that looks just as good as
the one in today's experiment

623
00:25:17,310 --> 00:25:21,820
out of everyday things
you find around the house.

624
00:25:21,820 --> 00:25:24,320
Exactly, and who
wants to go first?

625
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:25,320
Oh! Oh!

626
00:25:25,320 --> 00:25:27,320
- I'll go first, I'll go.
- Okay, Gypsy.

627
00:25:27,320 --> 00:25:29,830
You go first, alright
this is yours, boy,

628
00:25:29,820 --> 00:25:32,320
looks like you put a lot of
work into it, what'd you use?

629
00:25:32,330 --> 00:25:34,830
I used a Quaker Oats box,

630
00:25:34,830 --> 00:25:35,830
Uh huh.

631
00:25:35,830 --> 00:25:37,830
- Some of my old earrings.
- Mm-hmm.

632
00:25:37,830 --> 00:25:39,840
A sneaker and some
carpet pieces left over

633
00:25:39,830 --> 00:25:42,830
from when I single-handedly
remodeled my room.

634
00:25:42,840 --> 00:25:44,840
Oh, that is great.

635
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:46,340
You know what? I'm gonna put
that on the refrigerator.

636
00:25:46,340 --> 00:25:47,340
Oh!

637
00:25:47,340 --> 00:25:48,610
And we have Tom's over here.

638
00:25:48,610 --> 00:25:52,120
Looks like he's got a
Clorox bottle, is it?

639
00:25:52,110 --> 00:25:53,610
Yeah, it's a
spaceship but I suppose

640
00:25:53,610 --> 00:25:56,610
you could use it as a rock salt
scoop or whatever, I dunno.

641
00:25:56,620 --> 00:25:59,120
Doesn't sound like
you're too into it, Tom.

642
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:01,120
Okay, draw two nose holes
on it, it's a piggy bank.

643
00:26:01,120 --> 00:26:05,130
Oh Crow T. Robot,
would you look at this?

644
00:26:05,130 --> 00:26:06,130
Wow.

645
00:26:06,130 --> 00:26:07,630
This is exemplary, Crow.

646
00:26:07,630 --> 00:26:10,130
It's a scale model of
the Satellite of Love

647
00:26:10,130 --> 00:26:13,140
and it is fantastic,
how did you do it, Crow?

648
00:26:13,130 --> 00:26:16,630
String, toothpicks, eggshells
and baking powder paste.

649
00:26:16,640 --> 00:26:19,140
I'm gonna put lighter fluid on
it and burn it in the driveway.

650
00:26:19,140 --> 00:26:21,650
Well, it just goes to
show you that you can make

651
00:26:21,640 --> 00:26:23,640
a spaceship model
out of anything.

652
00:26:23,640 --> 00:26:25,140
Here's mine right here.

653
00:26:25,150 --> 00:26:26,150
(Tom and Crow scoffing)

654
00:26:26,150 --> 00:26:27,650
(laughing) Look at
that piece of crap!

655
00:26:27,650 --> 00:26:30,650
What'd you use here, a
bowling pin, lacrosse helmet,

656
00:26:30,650 --> 00:26:32,660
some Tupperware panels, I--

657
00:26:32,650 --> 00:26:34,650
Wait a minute.

658
00:26:34,660 --> 00:26:35,660
(coughing)

659
00:26:35,660 --> 00:26:37,160
- Commercial sign.
- Oh.

660
00:26:37,160 --> 00:26:38,160
- Commercial sign.
- Oh yeah,

661
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:39,160
commercial sign.

662
00:26:39,160 --> 00:26:41,160
(robots laughing and talking)

663
00:26:41,160 --> 00:26:47,040
("Love Theme from MST3K")

664
00:26:47,030 --> 00:26:48,530
(men talking)

665
00:26:48,540 --> 00:26:52,040
(robots whistling)
Whew, that was close.

666
00:26:52,040 --> 00:26:54,040
They all sent in "Northern
Exposure" spec scripts.

667
00:26:54,040 --> 00:26:55,550
That Munson's record is as
clean as mine, maybe cleaner.

668
00:26:55,540 --> 00:26:57,540
All three of them were
excellent security risks.

669
00:26:57,550 --> 00:26:59,050
It's a familiar pattern,

670
00:26:59,050 --> 00:27:01,050
top scientist engaged in
top level research work,

671
00:27:01,050 --> 00:27:04,550
suddenly walks off with
valuable data and materials.

672
00:27:04,550 --> 00:27:05,060
[Tom, Joel, And
Crow] Hugh Beaumont!

673
00:27:05,050 --> 00:27:06,050
But why?

674
00:27:06,050 --> 00:27:08,050
Who's behind all this?

675
00:27:08,060 --> 00:27:10,060
Well, at least we know
it's not the Russians.

676
00:27:10,060 --> 00:27:11,560
They already have the technology

677
00:27:11,560 --> 00:27:13,560
to produce the same material.

678
00:27:13,560 --> 00:27:14,560
An Irving Thalberg award.

679
00:27:14,560 --> 00:27:16,070
- I know it's a long shot,
- Hmm.

680
00:27:16,060 --> 00:27:18,060
But I'm beginning to
suspect red China's our heavy.

681
00:27:18,070 --> 00:27:21,070
The closest these have
ever been to anything Chinese

682
00:27:21,070 --> 00:27:23,070
is the local chop suey house.

683
00:27:23,070 --> 00:27:23,570
(Crow laughing)

684
00:27:23,570 --> 00:27:24,070
Shut up!

685
00:27:24,570 --> 00:27:25,080
Then who?

686
00:27:25,570 --> 00:27:26,570
We've got to come up
with something and quick,

687
00:27:26,570 --> 00:27:27,570
Washington's breathing
down my neck.

688
00:27:28,080 --> 00:27:29,080
The question is,

689
00:27:29,080 --> 00:27:31,080
which one of these
is gonna be next?

690
00:27:31,080 --> 00:27:33,580
Take your pick, they're
all above suspicion.

691
00:27:33,580 --> 00:27:36,590
I've had them under
surveillance for over a month.

692
00:27:36,580 --> 00:27:38,580
Oh, here's one

693
00:27:38,590 --> 00:27:40,590
I wouldn't mind having
under surveillance.

694
00:27:40,590 --> 00:27:43,590
That's my daughter,
you bastard.

695
00:27:43,590 --> 00:27:45,100
Dr. Lynn Young.

696
00:27:45,090 --> 00:27:46,590
- Yum yum.
- Clinical research

697
00:27:46,590 --> 00:27:48,090
division of Pacific
Laboratories.

698
00:27:48,100 --> 00:27:49,600
Oh, she's top drawer.

699
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:51,600
She's here from Taiwan, as a
special favor to the nationals.

700
00:27:52,100 --> 00:27:53,600
(buzzer ringing)

701
00:27:53,600 --> 00:27:55,110
Yeah, what is it now?

702
00:27:55,100 --> 00:27:56,100
Miss Wilson is here

703
00:27:56,100 --> 00:27:57,600
with the reports
from the crime lab.

704
00:27:57,610 --> 00:28:00,110
- Ann or Nancy?
- Send her in.

705
00:28:00,110 --> 00:28:02,110
Our troubles are over.

706
00:28:02,110 --> 00:28:05,620
Gale Wilson, number one
undercover girl is on the case.

707
00:28:05,610 --> 00:28:06,610
It's Mary Kay Ash.

708
00:28:06,610 --> 00:28:09,110
You have an eye for talent,
Mr. Martin...

709
00:28:09,120 --> 00:28:10,620
- ...but that's about all.
- Jamming our radar.

710
00:28:10,620 --> 00:28:12,620
You're way off
track in this case.

711
00:28:12,620 --> 00:28:13,620
That's what I love
about your dear,

712
00:28:13,620 --> 00:28:15,630
your respect for your superiors.

713
00:28:15,620 --> 00:28:17,120
Alright, Gale, what'd you get?

714
00:28:17,630 --> 00:28:18,630
Adenoids.

715
00:28:18,630 --> 00:28:20,130
It seems Dr.
Munson did not die

716
00:28:20,130 --> 00:28:22,630
from a fall over the cliff.

717
00:28:22,630 --> 00:28:23,630
Are you serious?

718
00:28:23,630 --> 00:28:25,140
With that hairdo?

719
00:28:25,130 --> 00:28:26,630
Here's the coroner's report.

720
00:28:26,630 --> 00:28:28,130
The corn's report?

721
00:28:28,140 --> 00:28:30,640
First he was shot
then he wasn't shot.

722
00:28:30,640 --> 00:28:32,140
(audience groaning)

723
00:28:32,140 --> 00:28:34,640
Only he wasn't killed, I
suppose now we'll discover

724
00:28:34,640 --> 00:28:36,150
he died from an overdose
of sleeping pills.

725
00:28:36,140 --> 00:28:38,140
No, he was electrocuted.

726
00:28:38,150 --> 00:28:39,150
D'oh!

727
00:28:39,150 --> 00:28:40,150
Electrocuted?

728
00:28:40,150 --> 00:28:42,150
Now look here, don't
you play games with me,

729
00:28:42,150 --> 00:28:44,650
I'll have you transferred
back to Washington, Miss Hart?

730
00:28:44,650 --> 00:28:46,660
On the level,
see for yourself.

731
00:28:46,650 --> 00:28:49,150
Dr. Munson was dead
at least 12 hours

732
00:28:49,160 --> 00:28:50,660
before they found
him in the canyon.

733
00:28:50,660 --> 00:28:52,160
Look could we
please just not fight?

734
00:28:52,160 --> 00:28:54,160
- Found my house on here.
- Alright, Glenn

735
00:28:54,160 --> 00:28:55,170
Bite me.

736
00:28:55,660 --> 00:28:56,660
Suppose you tell me how a man

737
00:28:56,660 --> 00:28:58,660
who's been dead
for several hours

738
00:28:58,670 --> 00:29:01,170
can drive a car up a
mountain road, get out,

739
00:29:01,170 --> 00:29:02,170
and jump over a cliff?

740
00:29:02,170 --> 00:29:03,670
He's Jesus?

741
00:29:03,670 --> 00:29:04,670
He was murdered,
driven there by somebody,

742
00:29:05,170 --> 00:29:06,670
and thrown over the cliff.

743
00:29:06,670 --> 00:29:08,670
What a wonderful deduction!

744
00:29:08,680 --> 00:29:09,680
Thank you.

745
00:29:09,680 --> 00:29:11,180
You Mr. Cole or Mr. Martin?

746
00:29:11,180 --> 00:29:12,680
Cole.

747
00:29:12,680 --> 00:29:14,180
You should have
been a detective.

748
00:29:14,180 --> 00:29:15,190
D'oh!

749
00:29:15,180 --> 00:29:16,180
(Tom mimicking rimshot)

750
00:29:16,180 --> 00:29:17,180
Well I suppose we'll
discover the man who was shot

751
00:29:17,180 --> 00:29:20,180
by the guard at the research
lab was Dr. Munson's

752
00:29:20,190 --> 00:29:22,190
non-existent twin brother.

753
00:29:22,190 --> 00:29:23,190
I'm kidding, of course.

754
00:29:23,190 --> 00:29:25,200
See what the
research department has

755
00:29:25,190 --> 00:29:26,690
on the Dornheimer estate.

756
00:29:26,690 --> 00:29:29,190
It's a mansion on
the Palisades here.

757
00:29:29,200 --> 00:29:30,700
Professor Dornheimer?

758
00:29:30,700 --> 00:29:31,700
Who's he?

759
00:29:31,700 --> 00:29:32,200
A famous physicist.

760
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:33,700
Your Prozac, sir.

761
00:29:33,700 --> 00:29:34,700
He used to be on
the space agency

762
00:29:34,700 --> 00:29:36,210
advisory board until recently.

763
00:29:36,700 --> 00:29:38,200
Now he's retired.

764
00:29:38,210 --> 00:29:40,210
He acts as a
sort of a consultant

765
00:29:40,210 --> 00:29:41,210
to the younger generation.

766
00:29:41,210 --> 00:29:44,210
If a genius has a problem, he
goes to see the old professor.

767
00:29:44,210 --> 00:29:45,220
I'm checking out.

768
00:29:45,210 --> 00:29:47,710
He'd know all the leading
scientists personally.

769
00:29:47,720 --> 00:29:49,220
Probably.
Why, what's on your mind?

770
00:29:49,220 --> 00:29:52,220
Well you just said it,
when a genius has a problem,

771
00:29:52,220 --> 00:29:53,720
he goes to see
the old professor.

772
00:29:53,720 --> 00:29:55,730
Hey, what about
our luncheon date?

773
00:29:55,720 --> 00:29:57,220
It is now a dinner date.

774
00:29:57,220 --> 00:29:58,720
I already have a dinner date.

775
00:29:58,730 --> 00:30:00,230
Break it.

776
00:30:00,230 --> 00:30:01,730
Geez, what a dickweed.

777
00:30:01,730 --> 00:30:03,730
That's what I love about him.

778
00:30:03,730 --> 00:30:06,740
He's so modest, so considerate,

779
00:30:06,730 --> 00:30:09,730
and so sure of himself.

780
00:30:09,740 --> 00:30:10,740
And so sure of you.

781
00:30:10,740 --> 00:30:11,740
Prozac?

782
00:30:12,240 --> 00:30:13,240
Guests of the
Human Duplicators

783
00:30:13,740 --> 00:30:15,750
stay at the Airport
Inn on the BeltLine.

784
00:30:15,740 --> 00:30:21,740
(ominous music)

785
00:30:21,750 --> 00:30:23,750
What's this?

