00:39:53,520 --> 00:39:55,520
I hate that I know that.

1049
00:39:55,520 --> 00:39:59,030
Jim Henson's Jack
Palance Babies.

1050
00:39:59,030 --> 00:40:01,530
Ah, Phil Harris
and Bubbles Rothermere

1051
00:40:01,530 --> 00:40:04,530
in the background, for
those playing along at home.

1052
00:40:04,530 --> 00:40:07,040
You guys think that
Phil Harris drank?

1053
00:40:07,030 --> 00:40:08,530
No.

1054
00:40:08,540 --> 00:40:12,540
- No?
- Uh, I don't know.

1055
00:40:12,540 --> 00:40:14,040
Look, why don't you
just do it in the bus tub,

1056
00:40:14,040 --> 00:40:16,550
for crying out loud?

1057
00:40:16,540 --> 00:40:20,040
Now, right now.

1058
00:40:20,050 --> 00:40:23,050
I crap bigger than them.

1059
00:40:23,050 --> 00:40:25,550
Visit Syria?

1060
00:40:25,550 --> 00:40:30,560
(robots singing upbeat tune)

1061
00:40:30,560 --> 00:40:35,060
Hey Bob, is this
raw bacon for me?

1062
00:40:35,060 --> 00:40:37,570
That's a Pedro Almodovar film.

1063
00:40:37,560 --> 00:40:41,060
He's doing a translation
of the Kama Sutra.

1064
00:40:41,070 --> 00:40:41,570
Glenn?

1065
00:40:42,070 --> 00:40:42,570
(Crow mocking Gale's voice)

1066
00:40:42,570 --> 00:40:45,070
One thing I don't understand,

1067
00:40:45,070 --> 00:40:48,080
If Professor Dornheimer's the
mastermind of these robberies,

1068
00:40:48,070 --> 00:40:50,070
what is he doing
with the materials?

1069
00:40:50,080 --> 00:40:51,580
You think he's
shipping them abroad?

1070
00:40:51,580 --> 00:40:53,580
Why do I sound
like Polly Holiday?

1071
00:40:53,580 --> 00:40:55,580
Glenn, I'm talking to you.

1072
00:40:55,580 --> 00:40:56,090
Yeah, I thought so.

1073
00:40:56,580 --> 00:40:57,580
Well, say something.

1074
00:40:57,580 --> 00:40:58,580
Something.

1075
00:40:58,590 --> 00:40:59,590
Something.

1076
00:40:59,590 --> 00:41:00,590
D'oh!

1077
00:41:00,590 --> 00:41:01,590
- D'oh.
- Sometimes I wonder

1078
00:41:01,590 --> 00:41:02,590
what I see in you.

1079
00:41:02,590 --> 00:41:03,590
Got riff back on that.

1080
00:41:03,590 --> 00:41:04,590
(Glenn talking)

1081
00:41:04,590 --> 00:41:06,100
Me.

1082
00:41:06,090 --> 00:41:08,590
Gale, what do you know
about cybernetics?

1083
00:41:08,600 --> 00:41:09,600
Cyber who?

1084
00:41:09,600 --> 00:41:11,100
Cybernetics.

1085
00:41:11,100 --> 00:41:12,600
It's the study of
calculators and computers,

1086
00:41:12,600 --> 00:41:17,110
all kinds of mechanical
devices to control automation.

1087
00:41:17,100 --> 00:41:18,100
Mechanical brains.

1088
00:41:18,100 --> 00:41:20,100
So, cybernetics?

1089
00:41:20,110 --> 00:41:23,610
So, Dornheimer is the
chief exponent in the field.

1090
00:41:23,610 --> 00:41:26,120
He's the father of
the thinking machine.

1091
00:41:26,110 --> 00:41:27,110
Come on, jump
over the couch now.

1092
00:41:27,110 --> 00:41:28,110
That's the trouble today.

1093
00:41:28,110 --> 00:41:29,610
It's getting to the
point where people

1094
00:41:29,620 --> 00:41:32,120
hardly have a chance
to think for themselves.

1095
00:41:32,120 --> 00:41:33,120
Could you not talk?

1096
00:41:33,120 --> 00:41:34,120
I don't get the connection.

1097
00:41:34,120 --> 00:41:35,120
Please?

1098
00:41:35,120 --> 00:41:36,130
You just said it.

1099
00:41:36,120 --> 00:41:37,120
I did?

1100
00:41:37,120 --> 00:41:39,120
Yeah, listen to
what Dornheimer says.

1101
00:41:39,630 --> 00:41:42,130
"And there's every reason
to believe that in time

1102
00:41:42,130 --> 00:41:43,630
"man will devise a method
whereby

1103
00:41:43,630 --> 00:41:45,130
"a mechanical brain system

1104
00:41:45,630 --> 00:41:48,640
"will be linked electronically
with a human nervous system

1105
00:41:48,640 --> 00:41:52,640
thereby creating a totally
independent thinking machine."

1106
00:41:52,640 --> 00:41:54,140
That'd be me, pretty much.

1107
00:41:54,140 --> 00:41:55,640
"Once this phase
has been accomplished,

1108
00:41:55,640 --> 00:41:58,650
"it will only be a short
step to the creation

1109
00:41:58,650 --> 00:42:00,650
of the ultimate in
mechanical devices,"

1110
00:42:00,650 --> 00:42:01,650
Me.

1111
00:42:01,650 --> 00:42:02,650
- "The android."
- No, me.

1112
00:42:02,650 --> 00:42:03,650
- No, me.
- Me.

1113
00:42:03,650 --> 00:42:04,150
- An android?
- Me.

1114
00:42:04,150 --> 00:42:05,150
Me.

1115
00:42:05,150 --> 00:42:06,160
What's an android?

1116
00:42:06,150 --> 00:42:08,650
An android dear, is a robot,

1117
00:42:08,660 --> 00:42:10,660
like a mechanical man.

1118
00:42:10,660 --> 00:42:11,660
Like my acting.

1119
00:42:11,660 --> 00:42:14,160
Except with one
important difference.

1120
00:42:14,160 --> 00:42:17,170
An android would look
exactly like a human being.

1121
00:42:17,160 --> 00:42:18,660
Except for the huge
bolts in the neck.

1122
00:42:19,170 --> 00:42:21,170
An android that looks
like a human being?

1123
00:42:21,170 --> 00:42:22,670
Like you or me?

1124
00:42:22,670 --> 00:42:26,180
Or Wells, or...
or Dr. Munson.

1125
00:42:26,170 --> 00:42:28,170
Oh, that's the wildest
thing I ever heard of.

1126
00:42:28,170 --> 00:42:30,670
Let's drink, then love.

1127
00:42:30,680 --> 00:42:32,680
Glenn, you don't believe
that's possible, do you?

1128
00:42:32,680 --> 00:42:34,680
Excuse me, I'm gonna go
toss myself out the window here.

1129
00:42:34,680 --> 00:42:35,180
Glenn?

1130
00:42:35,180 --> 00:42:36,190
(Tom mimicking glass breaking)

1131
00:42:36,180 --> 00:42:38,180
You don't believe that
Professor Dornheimer

1132
00:42:38,180 --> 00:42:40,680
has actually done this, do you?

1133
00:42:40,690 --> 00:42:43,190
Gale, all through
history there have been men

1134
00:42:43,190 --> 00:42:45,190
with minds like Dornheimer's.

1135
00:42:45,190 --> 00:42:46,700
Like Dornheimer died?

1136
00:42:46,690 --> 00:42:49,190
Men like that can go as far
as their minds can reach.

1137
00:42:49,200 --> 00:42:50,700
Anything they can see

1138
00:42:50,700 --> 00:42:52,200
I can see better.

1139
00:42:52,200 --> 00:42:53,700
(Tom laughing)

1140
00:42:53,700 --> 00:42:55,700
So, why not androids?

1141
00:42:55,700 --> 00:43:01,940
("Love Theme from MST3K")

1142
00:43:01,940 --> 00:43:04,450
Oh, this is beautiful.

1143
00:43:04,440 --> 00:43:06,940
That's me, and hey, that's me,

1144
00:43:06,950 --> 00:43:09,450
and look over there,
there I am! (laughing)

1145
00:43:09,450 --> 00:43:10,950
You know, there's a
whole lot of me in here,

1146
00:43:11,450 --> 00:43:13,960
truckloads of me
and more on the way.

1147
00:43:13,950 --> 00:43:16,450
Hi Tom.
Well, hello there Tom,

1148
00:43:16,460 --> 00:43:18,460
you're looking
pretty dapper today.

1149
00:43:18,960 --> 00:43:20,960
Oh, he's looking sickly.

1150
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:22,970
Well you're gonna get that,
lose two or three a day,

1151
00:43:22,960 --> 00:43:26,960
it's just part of the business,
you can't get too attached.

1152
00:43:26,970 --> 00:43:28,470
That's why it's
evil to duplicate,

1153
00:43:28,470 --> 00:43:30,970
everyone's an individual and
that's why it's a sacrilege

1154
00:43:30,970 --> 00:43:34,480
to think that you have
the right to... oh my God!

1155
00:43:34,470 --> 00:43:36,470
Easy fellas, easy, it's okay.

1156
00:43:36,480 --> 00:43:37,480
Hi guys, guess what?

1157
00:43:37,980 --> 00:43:39,480
Tom, what have you done?

1158
00:43:39,480 --> 00:43:40,980
Oh!

1159
00:43:40,980 --> 00:43:43,490
Just playing God, it's
going pretty well. (laughing)

1160
00:43:43,480 --> 00:43:44,980
Tom, you made dozens
and dozens of yourself,

1161
00:43:44,980 --> 00:43:46,480
I don't think that's right.

1162
00:43:46,990 --> 00:43:47,990
You know, you brought
up some very serious

1163
00:43:47,990 --> 00:43:50,490
psycho-moral
issues here, buster.

1164
00:43:50,490 --> 00:43:52,490
Frankly, I don't
see a problem, Joel.

1165
00:43:52,490 --> 00:43:54,500
I've just surrounded myself
with an endless horde

1166
00:43:54,490 --> 00:43:56,990
of mindless disciples ready
to go forth and do my bidding!

1167
00:43:57,000 --> 00:43:59,000
I guess I don't see a downside.

1168
00:43:59,000 --> 00:44:00,500
How'd you do it anyway?

1169
00:44:00,500 --> 00:44:02,000
Oh, you know, simple prototype,

1170
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:05,010
Japanese management style,
just-in-time stock procedures.

1171
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:07,500
Oh hey, we've got more out
back, where do you want 'em?

1172
00:44:07,510 --> 00:44:09,010
Uh, just make sure
they're all signed in.

1173
00:44:09,010 --> 00:44:10,510
Roger.

1174
00:44:10,510 --> 00:44:12,510
Oh, Tom, listen, this is wrong.

1175
00:44:12,510 --> 00:44:15,520
I'm not gonna take care of
'em, I'm not gonna feed 'em,

1176
00:44:15,520 --> 00:44:17,020
and keep 'em out of
the kitchen, okay?

1177
00:44:17,020 --> 00:44:18,020
(Tom sighing)

1178
00:44:18,020 --> 00:44:19,020
Tom, Tom, Tom.

1179
00:44:19,020 --> 00:44:20,020
Once again you
really didn't think

1180
00:44:20,520 --> 00:44:21,520
this thing through, did ya?

1181
00:44:21,520 --> 00:44:25,030
Like you're so cool that
we need more of you.

1182
00:44:25,020 --> 00:44:27,020
Oh, cynics, huh?

1183
00:44:27,030 --> 00:44:29,030
Alright, Toms, attack!

1184
00:44:29,030 --> 00:44:30,030
Tear 'em apart, get 'em!

1185
00:44:30,030 --> 00:44:31,530
Rip 'em apart!

1186
00:44:31,530 --> 00:44:33,540
Come on, attack, come on,
you're embarrassing me.

1187
00:44:33,530 --> 00:44:34,530
(Crow laughing)
Tom.

1188
00:44:34,530 --> 00:44:36,530
Joel.

1189
00:44:36,540 --> 00:44:37,540
I guess you were right.

1190
00:44:37,540 --> 00:44:41,040
It is wrong to play God, I've
certainly learned my lesson.

1191
00:44:41,040 --> 00:44:45,050
I know one guy that needs
a time out. (trails off)

1192
00:44:45,040 --> 00:44:46,040
(Tom crying)

1193
00:44:46,050 --> 00:44:47,050
Well, what a lame thing.

1194
00:44:47,050 --> 00:44:48,050
(sighs)

1195
00:44:48,050 --> 00:44:49,050
Hey, hi.

1196
00:44:49,050 --> 00:44:49,550
You're kind of cute.

1197
00:44:49,550 --> 00:44:51,050
Oh, Joel?

1198
00:44:51,050 --> 00:44:52,050
(laughing)

1199
00:44:52,050 --> 00:44:53,060
Hey, what the, hey cut that out!

1200
00:44:53,550 --> 00:44:54,550
(alarm buzzing)

1201
00:44:54,550 --> 00:44:55,550
We got movie sign!

1202
00:44:55,560 --> 00:44:58,060
(screaming)

1203
00:44:58,560 --> 00:45:03,570
(doors creak and whir)

1204
00:45:03,560 --> 00:45:04,560
Just don't do it anymore.

1205
00:45:04,560 --> 00:45:06,530
I just don't want you--
just stop, okay?

1206
00:45:06,530 --> 00:45:09,530
Oh my god, oh it's terrible.

1207
00:45:09,540 --> 00:45:12,040
I'm with him.

1208
00:45:12,040 --> 00:45:15,040
Well.

1209
00:45:15,040 --> 00:45:18,550
Oh great, the
souvenir shop's closed.

1210
00:45:19,050 --> 00:45:21,550
Hey, give someone else
a chance at that ball.

1211
00:45:21,550 --> 00:45:25,050
Man he's got a real
cute shape on him.

1212
00:45:25,050 --> 00:45:26,560
I'm actually kind
of glad it's locked,

1213
00:45:26,550 --> 00:45:27,050
I'm pretty tired.

1214
00:45:27,050 --> 00:45:29,050
Now I can knock off.

1215
00:45:29,060 --> 00:45:32,060
I'm Hans Christian Andersen.

1216
00:45:32,060 --> 00:45:39,570
♪ Timothy, Timothy, hungry
as hell no food to eat.

1217
00:45:39,570 --> 00:45:41,070
Well here's something

1218
00:45:41,070 --> 00:45:42,070
that never happened
in his career.

1219
00:45:42,070 --> 00:45:46,070
(mimics drum roll)

1220
00:45:46,070 --> 00:45:49,080
Hey, it's got a loft!
Wow!

1221
00:45:49,080 --> 00:45:52,880
And torsos? Cool!

1222
00:45:52,880 --> 00:45:57,390
- Tony Bennett!
- ♪ I left my head...

1223
00:45:57,380 --> 00:46:00,380
Boy, if a fellow happened to be
a human duplicator,

1224
00:46:00,390 --> 00:46:05,390
he could have a ball up here!

1225
00:46:05,390 --> 00:46:07,400
Now, are these
natural formations?

1226
00:46:07,390 --> 00:46:09,890
Sacred miracle cave.

1227
00:46:09,900 --> 00:46:12,400
(laughing)

1228
00:46:12,400 --> 00:46:15,400
Say, nice gams.

1229
00:46:15,400 --> 00:46:18,410
Looks like he broke into
the display at Chess King.

1230
00:46:18,400 --> 00:46:18,900
(laughs)

1231
00:46:18,910 --> 00:46:23,910
Richard Kiel's got a
butt collection.

1232
00:46:23,910 --> 00:46:26,920
Tom Jones!
And Peter Garrett!

1233
00:46:26,910 --> 00:46:28,910
What do you know.

1234
00:46:28,920 --> 00:46:35,420
Put me down.

1235
00:46:35,420 --> 00:46:36,930
Uh, excuse me, do you work here?

1236
00:46:36,920 --> 00:46:39,420
Oh, no.

1237
00:46:39,430 --> 00:46:39,930
Mark Twain.

1238
00:46:39,930 --> 00:46:43,930
No, it's Hal
Holbrook, actually.

1239
00:46:43,930 --> 00:46:47,440
Look, no one's going
to tell you you need Clearasil.

1240
00:46:47,430 --> 00:46:48,930
Oh, just looking
for the bathroom.

1241
00:46:48,940 --> 00:46:51,940
Nothing, nothing. Oh.

1242
00:46:51,940 --> 00:46:54,940
Hold me wall, hold me tight.

1243
00:46:54,940 --> 00:46:57,450
If you don't move, you're
invisible. Remember that.

1244
00:46:57,940 --> 00:47:03,940
Mmm? Hmm.

1245
00:47:03,950 --> 00:47:12,460
Just in time, I'm starved.

1246
00:47:12,460 --> 00:47:16,470
How come I never get
nurses like that?

1247
00:47:16,460 --> 00:47:19,460
Hey, who's playing
that contrabassoon?

1248
00:47:19,470 --> 00:47:20,470
Ollie North?

1249
00:47:20,470 --> 00:47:27,980
Contra-bassoon.

1250
00:47:27,970 --> 00:47:32,970
Where angels go,
trouble follows.

1251
00:47:32,980 --> 00:47:34,480
Dom, we'll be back
in an a half an hour,

1252
00:47:34,480 --> 00:47:39,490
Mr. Deluise.

1253
00:47:39,490 --> 00:47:41,490
Well, back for another load.

1254
00:47:41,490 --> 00:47:43,990
You know, for nurses,
they're not very speedy.

1255
00:47:43,990 --> 00:47:50,500
That's why they
work in a morgue.

1256
00:47:50,500 --> 00:47:53,000
Hey they're hickory
smoking people in here.

1257
00:47:53,000 --> 00:47:54,500
Oh, fellow must
have been Jewish,

1258
00:47:54,500 --> 00:47:56,000
they buried him in a steam bath.

1259
00:47:56,000 --> 00:48:00,010
Zing.

1260
00:48:00,010 --> 00:48:02,510
Judy Carne?

1261
00:48:02,510 --> 00:48:10,020
I'm up, I'm up.

1262
00:48:10,020 --> 00:48:12,020
Too much porcellana.

1263
00:48:12,020 --> 00:48:16,530
Too bad, that's
her throwing hand.

1264
00:48:16,520 --> 00:48:19,020
(muffled) Hey, I'm in here.

1265
00:48:19,030 --> 00:48:25,530
Martin Sheen.

1266
00:48:25,530 --> 00:48:31,040
Then he found one
that was just right.

1267
00:48:31,040 --> 00:48:32,540
Ew, that guy's ripe.

1268
00:48:32,540 --> 00:48:35,540
Ah, I think there's a
pattern developing here.

1269
00:48:35,540 --> 00:48:39,550
There's people in these things.

1270
00:48:39,550 --> 00:48:43,550
Uh, this one's gone over.

1271
00:48:43,550 --> 00:48:48,060
They should label those.

1272
00:48:48,050 --> 00:48:50,550
Yeah, you know it's always
the last place you look.

1273
00:48:50,560 --> 00:48:53,060
The Doctor will see you now.

1274
00:48:53,560 --> 00:48:55,060
Shattered, shattered.

1275
00:48:55,060 --> 00:48:59,070
Shadoobie...
Shatter.

1276
00:48:59,070 --> 00:49:00,570
Clean up in aisle three.

1277
00:49:00,570 --> 00:49:01,070
(laughs)

1278
00:49:01,070 --> 00:49:02,070
I'm kidding.

1279
00:49:02,070 --> 00:49:02,570
Zing.

1280
00:49:02,570 --> 00:49:04,570
Cause...

1281
00:49:04,570 --> 00:49:10,080
Well, that was a cool exhibit.
What's next, I wonder.

1282
00:49:10,080 --> 00:49:14,580
Tickets please, tickets
please, tickets please.

1283
00:49:14,580 --> 00:49:19,590
Well he's a queer duck.

1284
00:49:19,590 --> 00:49:22,590
Huh, the Guthrie Lab.

1285
00:49:22,590 --> 00:49:27,100
A wall of ginger snap.

1286
00:49:27,090 --> 00:49:31,590
It's Malibu Barbie
torture chambers.

1287
00:49:31,600 --> 00:49:38,610
Hi I am new Asian Barbie.

1288
00:49:38,600 --> 00:49:41,100
That was great pink
lady, we're going

1289
00:49:41,110 --> 00:49:46,620
to lay down the
rhythm track now.

1290
00:49:46,610 --> 00:49:50,110
Hey, I have no idea
what I'm doing here.

1291
00:49:50,120 --> 00:49:54,120
And the phone lines
are going crazy.

1292
00:49:54,120 --> 00:49:57,630
Let's take it from the top,
a little more bass this time.

1293
00:49:57,620 --> 00:49:59,120
It stinks.

1294
00:49:59,130 --> 00:50:02,130
I'm going to take my
lunch break now, excuse me.

1295
00:50:02,630 --> 00:50:03,630
Oh, she always takes her break

1296
00:50:03,630 --> 00:50:05,630
no matter what--
Doctor.

1297
00:50:05,630 --> 00:50:11,640
♪ Oh, dream weaver,
oh, Dennis Weaver.

1298
00:50:11,640 --> 00:50:14,640
(laughs)

1299
00:50:15,140 --> 00:50:17,650
She's turning
off the gym lights.

1300
00:50:17,640 --> 00:50:23,140
Oh, she's cooking up just fine.

1301
00:50:23,150 --> 00:50:27,160
It's like Rick Wakeman.

1302
00:50:27,150 --> 00:50:31,150
Well, kind of close, I suppose.

